WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.280
Carvana.&nbsp;

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:05.120
The strange used car dealership…&nbsp;
with a giant vending machine.&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:12.560
A company that exploded, almost collapsed, then&nbsp;
resurged even stronger, even joining the S&amp;P 500.&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.040
But, it might all be fraud.
Since our last video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.600
things have gotten much worse.
At least six different lawsuits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.920
and even the SEC is assaulting the company&nbsp;
with investigations, lawsuits, and accusations.&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.320
Accusations of insider trading, inflated&nbsp;
revenue, moving risky loans around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.920
and misleading investors.
“Unbeknownst to investors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.800
Carvana’s sustainable growth machine was&nbsp;
built on a fraudulent pump-and-dump scheme.”&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:50.480
Carvana might just be one giant scheme to&nbsp;
enrich these two men, at everyone’s expense.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.520
If you’ve never heard of this company, Carvana&nbsp;
is basically just, a fancy used car dealership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.600
with a gimmick. That giant vending machine.
They prioritized an online, fully 3d shopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.320
experience, and let customers deposit&nbsp;
a large coin to retrieve their car.&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.200
And they’re currently fighting a giant lawsuit,&nbsp;
from multiple law firms, and shareholders.&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.040
Things are so bad, that the SEC&nbsp;
has ordered Carvana to present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:27.360
documents for an ongoing, formal investigation.
So why is this happening? And what did Carvana do?&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.720
The two most important people in this story,&nbsp;
are CEO Ernest Garcia III, and his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.400
Ernest Garcia II, a major shareholder.
And this all might be one big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.960
pump and dump, to make them rich.
It starts with Carvana’s rather predatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.920
business model, which unfortunately,&nbsp;
has led a lot of people into a trap.&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.360
Carvana exploded not just because of this vending&nbsp;
machine, or their online shopping experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.680
but because of their financing.
Carvana intentionally attracts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.720
customers with low credit. Those who might&nbsp;
not have a ton of options to get a car.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.400
Someone left this very insightful&nbsp;
comment on our previous video:&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.600
”I worked as an underwriter for Carvana,&nbsp;
and they're one of the most predatory car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.920
companies. At the time, I believe the highest&nbsp;
interest rate you could get was 28.6%. I'd say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.480
that 60% of their customers have poor&nbsp;
credit, and so they're gonna end up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:29.280
the 15-20+ % range. “So most customers will&nbsp;
get a $15k car, and because of the interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.720
it's closer to $18-$20k.””
This checks out. “Almost 44%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:42.080
of Carvana’s loans it sells in ABS deals are&nbsp;
non-prime. Over 80% of its recent non-prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:48.560
ABS deals have weighted average FICO scores in&nbsp;
the “deep subprime” range, the riskiest levels,”&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.640
This was especially bad with Carvana’s&nbsp;
reputation for just sending out faulty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.480
cars to these more vulnerable customers.
but what we’re interested in, are those loans.&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.040
This is the key to understanding Carvana’s&nbsp;
entire scheme, and why they’re being accused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.600
of misleading investors, big time.
In the pandemic, getting a car was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:14.000
cheap. Very cheap. The Federal Reserve dropped&nbsp;
interest rates from 1.75%, to 1.25%, then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:20.960
just two weeks later, all the way to 0% to 0.25%.
Not only that, since supply chains were strained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.040
there were fewer cars, so prices&nbsp;
for new and used cars, went up.&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.120
It was cheaper to finance a car,&nbsp;
yet cars had a higher price.&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.040
All of this, was perfect for Carvana.
You see, Carvana isn’t just a “used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.480
car company”, in fact, you could argue&nbsp;
they’re more a “loan selling company”.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.560
Carvana would attract more customers with&nbsp;
bad credit, which have high interest rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.320
making the loans more valuable.
They would then bundle those loans together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.800
and sell them to third parties, who want&nbsp;
to make money on that high interest.&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.120
So Carvana’s actual cash engine&nbsp;
here, isn’t the car customer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.640
it’s whoever buys that giant bundle of loans.
That’s not illegal, in fact it’s quite normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:07.040
for used car companies. Carvana sells&nbsp;
the car, the customer gets the car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.080
and a third party gets profit&nbsp;
from interest on the loan.&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.080
The problem is who Carvana&nbsp;
was selling these loans to.&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.760
But to understand that, we need to look at&nbsp;
why Carvana crashed so hard, right here.&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:30.640
And why shareholders are now suing them,&nbsp;
and why even the SEC is investigating them.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.960
As you might have guessed, that&nbsp;
pandemic boom didn’t last forever.&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.720
Carvana’s stock price began to plummet.&nbsp;
From $337 in July 2021, all the way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:47.520
$4.7 in December the following year.
A 98% drop… yeah.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.120
Used cars began to drop in&nbsp;
price as the world opened up.&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.440
Prices went down, and financing&nbsp;
cars got much more expensive.&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.912
But Carvana, to keep up with demand, had&nbsp;
bought a lot of used cars, at a high price.&nbsp;

00:05:00.912 --> 00:05:02.160
[Neil Saunders, Managing Director,&nbsp;
GlobalData]: “The year has generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.280
been poor for Carvana with losses mounting&nbsp;
and sales declining. Much of this is related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.360
to external economic factors, which&nbsp;
is pushing down demand for vehicles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:17.280
Carvana failed to anticipate this which meant it&nbsp;
had far too much inventory, especially earlier in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.160
the year. This dragged down profit,”
They had also taken on a ton of debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.320
acquiring some other companies,&nbsp;
despite not being profitable.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.520
Carvana was now on the verge of bankruptcy.
And, this was particularly bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:38.080
because Carvana was way, way overvalued.
It was also “trading at an 845% higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.800
sales multiple relative to online&nbsp;
car peers CarMax and AutoNation".

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.600
But it gets so much worse.
In 2022, a number of law firms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:53.360
filed lawsuits against Carvana on behalf&nbsp;
of investors, all with the same accusation.&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.280
“Defendants billed Carvana to&nbsp;
investors as an ecommerce company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:02.560
akin to the Amazon of the used car industry.&nbsp;
Carvana would be a seemingly limitless growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:07.040
machine because the Company’s disruptive&nbsp;
model was full of competitive advantages”.&nbsp;

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:12.160
“Unbeknownst to investors, Carvana’s&nbsp;
sustainable growth machine was a lemon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.720
built on a fraudulent pump-and-dump scheme&nbsp;
to boost Carvana’s retail sales growth.&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:20.960
Carvana and the executives filed&nbsp;
motions to dismiss this in 2024.&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.240
But, this was denied.
Carvana tried again:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:31.200
motions to dismiss in 2025. They’re denied again.
So, how did this pump and dump scheme work?&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.840
There is a long list of components, but most&nbsp;
of it boils down to not telling investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.800
about the risks the company was facing,&nbsp;
and the profitability of each sold car.&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.080
[Carvana] reported positive Retail GPU in each&nbsp;
of Carvana’s earnings calls and shareholder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.040
letters and assured investors that its&nbsp;
retail vehicle sales were profitable.&nbsp;

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:55.360
But, these cars were nowhere near as&nbsp;
profitable as they said they were.&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.960
Carvana’s actual per-vehicle profitability –&nbsp;
or lack thereof – by: (1) excluding certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.040
per vehicle operations expenses&nbsp;
from its calculation of Retail GPU;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.760
and (2) not disclosing or quantifying these&nbsp;
excluded costs separately so investors could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.080
decipher Carvana’s “unit economics” (i.e., overall&nbsp;
profitability) of retail sales on their own.&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:18.640
Carvana intentionally hid the full&nbsp;
cost of selling cars. Material costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:24.080
shipping cost, title and registration cost.
Which, by the way, is an entirely different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.720
scandal, where Carvana just wouldn’t register&nbsp;
sold cars, leaving customers stuck with temporary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:34.720
license plates, for months or even a year. Another&nbsp;
part of this big lawsuit. (show page 8 pdf)&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:40.640
Carvana also started hiding metrics like “average&nbsp;
days to sale”, important because it relates to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:45.920
car’s appreciation. They said this was because&nbsp;
of “the relative stability of average days to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:49.680
sale over the past three years.”
But, with the pandemic car price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:56.720
spikes and crashes… yeah that was a lie.
Shareholders didn’t have the full picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.800
and were intentionally misled when&nbsp;
Carvana said everything’s going great!&nbsp;

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:05.840
They also hid the fact that Carvana was pumping&nbsp;
sales growth by selling “in violation of title and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.560
registration laws and regulations” and pumping&nbsp;
sales by selling to customers further away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.520
which makes cars even less profitable.
But, we’re not even done.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.560
Remember Garcia II? The major Carvana&nbsp;
shareholder, and father of the CEO?&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.280
Well, he’s not allowed to run, or even&nbsp;
be employed by any company listed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:32.880
the NYSE. Which Carvana is listed on.
Why? He’s a federal felon, for bank fraud.&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.440
So what did he do?
“He installed his son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.360
as the Company’s Chief Executive Officer&nbsp;
(“CEO”) and Chairman, designed Carvana’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.840
voting structure so that he maintained more than&nbsp;
80% of the voting power and thus, controlled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:49.200
Carvana and its operations, and appointed his&nbsp;
loyal friends (one of whom previously had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:54.720
censured by the NYSE for actions he took on&nbsp;
behalf of Garcia Senior) as board members.&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:59.680
You literally can’t make this up.
Between 2020 and 2021, in the height&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:07.083
of Carvana’s growth, both Ernest Garcia II and&nbsp;
his son sold about $3.6 billion of Carvana stock.&nbsp;

00:09:07.083 --> 00:09:07.840
[Daniel Taylor, accounting professor at the&nbsp;
Wharton School of Business]:”What I’m saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:13.600
is the Garcias knew it was short-lived . . . .&nbsp;
The Garcias knew the music would eventually end.”&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:17.520
A stock which, they were pumping&nbsp;
up, by misleading investors.&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.480
Yet, even after all these&nbsp;
accusations, we’re still not done.&nbsp;

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:26.400
There’s something else the Garcias&nbsp;
were hiding, something massive.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.200
There are heaps of these&nbsp;
crazy busy stories out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.960
many you might never hear about.&nbsp;
And they have lots to teach us.

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:42.800
We cover stories like these every&nbsp;
week. If you like the sound of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.680
please subscribe! So you don’t miss an upload.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:51.280
On January 2nd, 2025, Hindenburg research&nbsp;
published a very thorough, detailed expose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:55.600
titled: Carvana: A Father-Son&nbsp;
Accounting Grift For The Ages.&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.160
They found that:
In 9 months of 2024,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:03.680
Carvana Sold $6.15 Billion In Loans&nbsp;
For A Gain Of $541 Million, A Metric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:09.520
Representing 2.2x Net Income In The Period.
Not all of this is suspected to be fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.520
but, $800 million of it was&nbsp;
from an “Unrelated Third Party”&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.000
It is highly suspected that Carvana sold&nbsp;
these loans to a company called Cerberus.&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.000
Their chairman is Dan Quayle,&nbsp;
one of Carvana’s directors.&nbsp;

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.440
Why does this matter?
Well for one, this makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:32.320
that “unrelated third party”, very much related.&nbsp;
And under SEC rules, this has to be disclosed.&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:37.120
And two, this means Carvana wasn’t&nbsp;
really “selling” these loans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:41.280
more just moving them elsewhere.
Or, as Hindenburg Research put it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.080
“Passing around risky loans like a hot potatoe.”
But it’s not just loans.&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:50.880
Over the last three fiscal years, Carvana has&nbsp;
generated $105 million revenue from selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:54.560
cars wholesale to DriveTime.
DriveTime is another used car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:58.320
dealership and finance company.
And who runs this company?&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:01.600
Ernest Garcia II.
That’s right.&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.640
Ernest Garcia III, also founded Carvana&nbsp;
originally as a DriveTime subsidiary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:11.840
before spinning it off as its own company.
A former Carvana leader confirmed that “warranty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:17.360
reimbursements from related-party DriveTime were&nbsp;
“pretty generous… back to Carvana” as a way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.960
showing better revenue to public investors.”
[Former Carvana Director]: “As a related-party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:22.960
we’re [Carvana] able to kind&nbsp;
of have an agreement that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:25.440
favorable to pull as much of that profit forward.”
[Former Carvana director, wholesale inventory]:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:29.680
“[Selling cars to DriveTime is] a lever&nbsp;
that’s not talked about. It’s kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.480
like Fight Club… there’s certain things we don’t&nbsp;
talk about, and we don’t talk about DriveTime.”&nbsp;

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:39.520
Feels like there should be a movie about Carvana,&nbsp;
like The Big Short or The Wolf of Wall Street.&nbsp;

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:45.040
It was around this time that Carvana’s stock had&nbsp;
climbed back up, which was good for the Garcias.&nbsp;

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:51.520
In July 2023, after the crash, Garcia&nbsp;
III bought back $126 million in shares.&nbsp;

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:56.240
The very next day, Carvana announced&nbsp;
the “best quarter in company history”.&nbsp;

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:59.920
Then, when it began to climb for a&nbsp;
second time, Ernest Garcia II sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:06.160
an additional $1.4 billion in Carvana stock.
Is Carvana just one big giant pump and dump?&nbsp;

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:11.920
Sure seems that way, and now, things&nbsp;
have gone beyond just lawsuits.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.480
In June 2025, the SEC issued&nbsp;
a formal subpoena to Carvana.&nbsp;

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:19.920
That means the SEC is compelling&nbsp;
Carvana to present documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.520
relating to the Hindenburg claims.
Speaking of documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.920
let’s check in on that other lawsuit.
The plaintiffs, are also attempting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:35.200
get documents from Carvana. Namely 112,889&nbsp;
emails, from a specific point in time.&nbsp;

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:41.680
But Carvana failed to provide 90% of these.
The plaintiff’s attorney argued that “We were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:46.160
concerned that defendants might rig this process&nbsp;
in a way that was set up to fail,” believing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:51.040
the defense team has refused to use the proper&nbsp;
search terms required to find specific documents.&nbsp;

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:55.600
Carvana seemed to be intentionally unhelpful&nbsp;
in providing these, by using searches to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:59.840
bury the more incriminating documents.
So, the judge let the plaintiffs request&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:05.600
up to 250 specific emails at a time, meaning&nbsp;
Carvana can’t just hide these emails in searches.&nbsp;

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:10.560
Of course, this is going to&nbsp;
take a long, long time. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:16.400
at least Carvana can’t hide them anymore.
Carvana might be the strangest, albeit most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:19.920
interesting car dealership, ever.
The giant vending machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:25.360
the pump &amp; dump, the creative accounting.
I sure wouldn’t trust them with my money though.

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:29.840
Speaking of being untrustworthy, another&nbsp;
crazy, recent story comes from Klarna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:34.080
the buy now, pay later company.
At least, that’s what they were.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.880
They’re trying to move into the world of&nbsp;
regular banking, but the moves they’re making,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:43.360
aren’t making investors happy. Watch this&nbsp;
video next to learn the rest of the story.

